<Layout title="DJI O4 Arming with Raspberry Pi Pico — CopperTrace" description="A working Raspberry Pi Pico controller for DJI O4 ARM/DISARM state over UART/MSP, with custom firmware and repeatable bench testing.">
<article class="article"><div class="article-meta">Project 003 · Updated 13 October 2026 · Working</div><h1>DJI O4 arming with Raspberry Pi Pico</h1>
<p class="lead">Custom Raspberry Pi Pico firmware provides the ARM/DISARM state used by the DJI O4 bench setup. A wired UART/MSP connection brings the unit into a repeatable test state and supports integration with Linux-side test control.</p>
<div class="callout success"><strong>Working on the bench.</strong> Pico-to-O4 communication and arming/disarming behaviour have been demonstrated on real hardware.</div>
<h2>The problem</h2><p>RF experiments are much easier to compare when the transmitter starts from a known state every time. Manually powering, arming and configuring a test unit introduces variation and makes longer automated test sequences difficult to reproduce.</p>
<h2>How Pico provides the arming state</h2>
<p>The Pico runs a custom MSPv1 endpoint over UART at 115200 baud. It handles the FC-style messages used by the test setup and reports the armed or disarmed state to O4. The host interface provides status and test commands, allowing the serial exchange and state changes to be checked during a run.</p>
<h2>Architecture</h2><div class="diagram"><div class="diagram-title">O4 TEST-BENCH CONTROL</div><div class="diagram-row"><div class="diagram-node">Linux / Labrax</div></div><div class="diagram-arrow">↓</div><div class="diagram-row"><div class="diagram-node diagram-accent">Raspberry Pi Pico</div></div><div class="diagram-arrow">↓</div><div class="diagram-row diagram-split"><div class="diagram-node">Power relay / cutoff</div><div class="diagram-node">UART / MSP · ARM / DISARM</div></div><div class="diagram-arrow">↘ &nbsp;&nbsp; controlled test state &nbsp;&nbsp; ↙</div><div class="diagram-row"><div class="diagram-node">DJI O4 test unit</div></div></div>
<h2>Confirmed on the bench</h2><div class="fact-grid"><div class="fact"><strong>Controller</strong><span>Dedicated Raspberry Pi Pico</span></div><div class="fact"><strong>Serial link</strong><span>UART, 115200 8N1</span></div><div class="fact"><strong>Arming interface</strong><span>ARM/DISARM via MSPv1</span></div><div class="fact"><strong>Safety</strong><span>Independent hardware power cutoff</span></div></div>
<p>The controller can reproduce arming/disarming behaviour used by the test setup and provides a deterministic way to bring the O4 unit into a known bench state. The setup also demonstrated voltage telemetry in the goggles and a working DisplayPort OSD path used during development.</p>
<h2>Integration with the automated bench</h2><p>The automated test rig combines the Pico serial controller with relay-based power control and an independent hardware cutoff. This gives repeatable runs with defined start and stop conditions. The arming interface is a separate part of that wider test arrangement.</p>
<h2>What this enables</h2><p>With power and control under software supervision, a Linux host can run repeatable experiments, associate captures with known transmitter state and retain evidence from each test. This is more useful for Sky Serpent development than manually operating the transmitter for every capture.</p>
<div class="callout"><strong>Project result:</strong> a working Pico-based arming interface for O4, with serial diagnostics and a repeatable path into a known bench state.</div>
